course: dictionary
lesson: 4575
title: Hello and greetings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: «Hello» is a small word with several jobs. You say it when you meet someone, when you answer the phone, and to get someone's attention. «Hello? Is anyone there?» is a question as well as a greeting, so the words around it show you the job it is doing.
explain: Hello can also be a noun, a greeting you give to someone. «Say hello to your mum» means pass on a greeting from you. Phrases like «say hello», «wave hello» and «hello again» are useful when you meet someone you have seen before.
explain: Good manners go with hello: look at the person, smile and say it clearly. On the phone, though, saying hello does not mean giving your name or address to someone you don't know. Being polite and being safe can both be true at the same time.
fact: «Hello» is an exclamation when you say it on its own, and a noun when you say «a hello» or «hellos».
fact: Hello is used to greet people, to answer the phone, and to get someone's attention.
fact: «Say hello to someone for me» means pass on a greeting to that person.
fact: Greetings differ around the world: «Namaste», with palms pressed together, is a respectful Indian and Nepali greeting.
fact: «As-salamu alaykum» means peace be upon you, and it is answered with «Wa alaykum as-salam» or simply hello.
exercise: «Hello? Is anyone there?» What job is the word hello doing here? | To spell a name | *To get someone's attention | To count the people in the room | To say goodbye
exercise: Mum says, «Say hello to Grandma for me.» What does she mean? | *Pass on a greeting to Grandma | Phone Grandma and ask for money | Tell Grandma to be quiet | Give Grandma a hug from the cat
exercise: «Shout hello in a big empty hall and a moment later the walls shout it back.» What is that returning sound called? | A greeting | A knock | *An echo | A whisper
test: When passing a horse and rider, why should you say hello? | *So the horse knows you are a person | So the horse will race you | So the rider will buy you lunch | So the horse goes to sleep
test: A cat trots in with its tail straight up like a flagpole. What is it saying? | Enough, I'm cross | It is asleep | Goodbye forever | *A happy hello
exam: On the phone with a caller you don't know, what should you avoid? | *Giving your name or address | Saying hello | Thanking them for calling | Asking who is calling
